  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 213 PM EST Thu Nov 5 2026\n\nMid-afternoon water vapor imagery shows a midlevel trough over the \nupper Great Lakes, with upstream ridging over the Rockies, northern \nPlains, and Canadian Prairies. Deep moisture is exiting eastward, \nwith drier air having made it into the western UP. A surface low \nanalyzed at 1002 mb near the Soo and high pressure building in from \nthe west is resulting in a strong pressure gradient, with 25-35 kt \nwind gusts common over inland areas, up to around 40 kt near the \nlakeshores. These winds should continue this afternoon before \ngradually decreasing this evening. Meanwhile, 850 mb temps are \nplummeting via strong northerly winds and should bottom out around -\n7C tonight. Blustery winds  This will set up delta-Ts in the -15 to \n-18 range, leading to a transition to lake effect rain (already \noccurring as of 2 pm). As temperatures gradually cool, expect rain \nshowers to mix with and change to snow showers mainly above 1000' \nelevation in the west and central UP this evening into tonight. \nForecast soundings show high RH in the boundary layer that extends \nup to roughly 1.5 km AGL...however, temperatures are quite marginal \nwith the top of that boundary layer only at around -10C and surface \ntemps only bottoming out around 30F in the coldest spots. Those air \ntemps, along with warm ground temps, should greatly limit \naccumulation potential despite prolonged shower activity overnight. \nWind fetch and moisture profiles favor the Michigamme Highlands for \naccumulating snow, with amounts of 2-3\" not out of the question per \nthe REFS probability matched mean. Otherwise, any accumulations \nshould be a dusting to around an inch, with travel impacts not \nexpected.\n\nLow-level moisture and rain/snow showers linger Friday in the \ncentral and east before building high pressure dries things out from \nwest to east. The first colder than normal night in some time is on \ntap Friday night with lows in the mid-teens to mid-20s. However, the \nnear to below normal temperatures will be short lived, as a strong \nPacific jet allows for the return of mild air flooding the CONUS and \nsouthern Canada. High temps are expected to rebound to slightly \nabove normal values Saturday with mid-40s to near 50, and well above \nnormal Sunday into Monday with highs well into the 50s.",
